    '=== PART 2 ===',
    'TITLE: Why Maps Never Tell the Whole Truth',
    'Cartographers are quick to admit that there is {{9|ANS:no|EXP:Fixed existential negation "there is no such thing as...".|TRAP:Writing "not", which cannot directly modify the noun phrase "such thing".}} such thing as an entirely objective map. Because the Earth is roughly spherical, its curved surface cannot be flattened onto a sheet of paper {{10|ANS:without|EXP:Preposition "without" followed by a gerund ("introducing") expresses an unavoidable accompaniment.|TRAP:Writing "not" or "unless", neither of which can precede a bare -ing form here.}} introducing distortion of area, shape, or distance. The sixteenth-century Mercator projection preserves compass bearings reliably, {{11|ANS:whereas/while/whilst|EXP:Contrastive conjunction joining two opposing finite clauses ("preserves bearings" vs. "exaggerates landmasses").|TRAP:Writing "however", which is a conjunctive adverb and creates a comma splice here.}} it dramatically exaggerates landmasses near the poles. {{12|ANS:So|EXP:C1 front-position inversion: "So + adverb/adjective + auxiliary + subject" ("So deeply ingrained has this visual bias become...").|TRAP:Writing "Such", which modifies a noun phrase ("Such a bias") rather than the adverb-adjective phrase "deeply ingrained".}} deeply ingrained has this visual bias become that even seasoned travellers are startled to learn that Africa is roughly fourteen times the size of Greenland.',
