    '=== PART 5 ===',
    'TITLE: The Myth of Effortless Originality in Architecture',
    'Walk into almost any architecture school critique and you will witness a curious ritual. Students pin up sweeping renderings of cantilevered timber pavilions or undulating glass towers, eager to prove that their design has sprung fully formed from an untamed imagination. When a visiting critic remarks that a floor plan echoes a seventeenth-century Venetian warehouse or a municipal bathhouse in Zurich, the student often winces, as though accused of petty theft. Somewhere in the mid-twentieth century, architecture inherited the romantic notion that genuine creativity demands a clean slate—an abrupt severance from everything built before.',
    '',
    'Historians of the built environment find this anxiety faintly ironic. For the overwhelming majority of human history, innovation in construction emerged precisely through disciplined imitation. Master builders copied proven roof spans, courtyard ratios, and stone-jointing techniques from neighbouring towns, modifying them incrementally to accommodate local winds, timber lengths, or shifts in civic ritual. What looks in hindsight like a bold stylistic leap was usually a pragmatic tweak forced by a stubborn hillside or a shortage of quarried limestone. Originality was a by-product of solving concrete problems, not a slogan printed on a studio wall.',
    '',
    'The modern fixation on signature silhouettes has carried tangible costs. Eager to deliver an unmistakable landmark that photographs effortlessly for international journals, cities have commissioned structures whose dramatic curves generate punishing solar glare, awkward interior corners, and eye-watering maintenance bills. Once the opening-week publicity subsides, facilities managers are left grappling with leaky skylights and bespoke cladding panels that no local contractor can replace. In such cases, the quest for instant visual novelty actively undermines the quieter virtues—durability, thermal comfort, adaptability—that determine whether a building is still loved fifty years later.',
